<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali-Shir Nava'i</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1441">1441</a>–<a href="/years/1501">1501</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Nawai, Ali-Shir Nawa'i</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali-Shir Nava'i was a poet, statesman and patron of the arts at the Timurid court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in the late fifteenth century. He wrote extensively in Chagatai Turkic and argued for its literary merit alongside Persian. He is regarded as a founding figure of Turkic literature and a key part of the cultural legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai.jpg" alt="Ali-Shir Nava'i" width="288" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qasim Ali · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mystics_in_a_garden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alisher Nava'i was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 1441 to a family of well-read Turkic chancery scribes, of the Chaghatai mir class of the Timurid elite. His father, Ghiyath al-Din Kichkina, served as a high-ranking officer in the palace of the Timurid ruler Shahrukh Mirza. After his father died, another ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, Abul-Qasim Babur Mirza, became the young man's guardian. Alisher was a schoolmate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara">Husayn Bayqara</a>, later sultan of Khorasan. He studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, Herat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, and when Husayn Bayqara seized power in Herat in 1469 he joined his service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1472 Alisher was appointed emir of the supreme council, the dīvān-i aʿlā. This brought him into conflict with the Persian bureaucrat Majd al-Din Muhammad Khvafi, whose centralising reforms threatened the privileges of the Turkic military elite. He stayed in Bayqara's service until his death on 3 January 1501, and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, and he never married. He is reported to have founded, restored or endowed some 370 mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, libraries, hospitals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and other institutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, among them the mausoleum of the poet Farid al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athtar">Attar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>. He backed historians such as Mirkhvand and Khvandamir, the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a> and musicians such as Husayn Udi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Under the pen name Nava'i, he wrote mainly in Chagatai Turkic, producing 30 works over 30 years, and also wrote in Persian as Fāni. His four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwans</a>, about 50,000 verses, correspond to the stages of life, from Wonders of Childhood to Benefits of Old Age. His Khamsa, five epic poems, answers that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a>, and Majalis al-Nafais collects over 450 biographical sketches of mostly contemporary poets. In Muhakamat al-Lughatayn he argued that Chagatai was superior to Persian for literary purposes, an uncommon view then.</p><p>Bernard Lewis called him the Chaucer of the Turks. His influence reached the Mughal founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, who wrote of his respect in his memoirs. Soviet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> sources regard him as an ethnic Uzbek and the founder of Uzbek literature, though applying modern ethnonyms to his time is anachronistic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali-Shir Nava'i” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The khanate also gave its name to Chagatai Turkic, the literary language of Central Asia in which <b>Ali-Shir Nava'i</b> wrote his poetry and Babur his memoirs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Timurid patronage also supported the Persian poetry of Jami and the works of <b>Ali-Shir Nava'i</b>, the greatest poet of Chaghatai Turkic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1480" aria-label="Herat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6132 920 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ali-Shir Nava'i"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6582 1190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1480">What was here in 1480? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
